<h2>The case</h2>
<p>The matter began as a missing-person complaint. On 26 March 2024, the sister of the prosecutrix reported her missing, and FIR No. 314/2024 was registered at Police Station Narela Industrial Area the following day. The girl was recovered in January 2025. Her account led to charges of a very different order: repeated sexual assault by several persons and her sale from hand to hand. The investigation arrayed twelve accused, and the case went to trial as SC No. 187/2025 before the Additional Sessions Judge (POCSO), Rohini Courts.</p>
<p>The applicant, in judicial custody since 6 March 2025, sought regular bail from the High Court under Section 483 read with Section 528 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023 — the provisions that have replaced Sections 439 and 482 of the CrPC.</p>

<h2>What weighed against release</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Gravity.</strong> The allegations are of gangrape of a minor and her sale — offences carrying some of the heaviest sentences in the statute book, including under Sections 4 and 6 of the POCSO Act.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The stage of trial.</strong> The prosecutrix&rsquo;s examination was still going on. The single most sensitive phase of a POCSO trial is the child&rsquo;s own deposition, and courts guard it closely.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Antecedents.</strong> The State pointed to earlier FIRs against the applicant under the Excise Act, undercutting the plea of clean antecedents.</div>
</div>
<p>On that matrix, the Court held that no case for bail was made out and dismissed the application, while making it clear that nothing said would affect the trial.</p>

<h2>The legal framework</h2>
<p>The order sits squarely within the Supreme Court&rsquo;s recent bail jurisprudence for grave offences. Once the trial has begun and material witnesses are being examined, the calculus changes: the question is no longer only custody versus liberty, but the integrity of the evidence being recorded. The Court drew on <em>State of Karnataka v. Sri Darshan</em>, (2026) 8 SCC 223, where the Supreme Court restated the considerations — gravity, role, stage of evidence, and the risk of witness tampering — and on its line of authority cautioning against release mid-deposition in serious crimes.</p>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Factor</th><th>How it played out here</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Nature of accusation</td><td>Gangrape and sale of a minor — aggravated offences under POCSO Sections 4 and 6 and IPC Section 376D</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Stage of trial</td><td>Prosecutrix under examination; her evidence incomplete</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Number of accused</td><td>Twelve, raising coordination and influence concerns</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Custody period</td><td>Since March 2025 — not yet long enough to outweigh the above</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>What this means in practice</h2>
<p>For the defence, the realistic window in such cases usually opens after the victim&rsquo;s evidence is complete: at that point the tampering apprehension loses much of its force, and prolonged custody with a slow-moving trial becomes the dominant consideration. Delhi High Court orders in recent months show both halves of this pattern — refusals while the child is deposing, and grants once the material witnesses have been recorded or have not supported the prosecution. The stage of evidence, more than any other single factor, decides these applications.</p>
<div class="note">This report summarises an order of the High Court of Delhi dated 3 November 2026 for general information. It is not legal advice, and it expresses no view on the pending trial.</div>
